## Step 2: Audit the MTN Attack Surface## 1. Look for Deep Link Schemes & Gateway Handlers
Fintech apps use intent filters and custom URLs to initiate payments or web views. Let's find out what deep links the MTN apps handle (e.g., momo://, mymtn://):

grep -E -i "scheme|host" com.mtn.mtncmr3_manifest.txt mtnft.momo.consumer_manifest.txt

## 2. Scan for Hardcoded API Endpoints, URLs, and Gateway IPs [4] 
Since you dumped the raw asset and binary resource strings using aapt dump strings, you can search the text directly for staging environments, API paths, or backend proxy domains:

grep -E -o "https?://[a-zA-Z0-9./?=&_-]+" com.mtn.mtncmr3_strings.txt mtnft.momo.consumer_strings.txt | sort -u > mtn_endpoints.txt
cat mtn_endpoints.txt

(Look out for keywords in the output like sandbox, staging, v1/api, or payment.)
## 3. Audit Over-Privileged Permissions
See what sensitive hardware and system features these apps are requesting within the Waydroid container:

grep -E "uses-permission" com.mtn.mtncmr3_manifest.txt mtnft.momo.consumer_manifest.txt

(Check if they request RECEIVE_SMS, READ_SMS, or READ_CONTACTS, which are highly sensitive for MoMo transactions).
